{
	// Cloudflare deploy config for the static site.
	//
	// Workers static assets rather than Cloudflare Pages: Pages' one real
	// advantage here would be Git-integrated builds, and those cannot work for
	// this project — `corpus/` is gitignored and lives only in a local
	// checkout, so a build triggered from the repo would find no corpus and
	// fall back to the two-book test fixtures (see site/README.md and
	// CLAUDE.md's warning about exactly this failure mode, which looks
	// confusing rather than obviously broken). Deploys come from a local
	// `npm run build` either way, which leaves Workers — the platform
	// Cloudflare is still developing — with no cost against it.
	//
	// `src/worker.ts` is deliberately tiny: it validates a request against the
	// corpus-generated route manifest, then hands static assets straight back
	// to the asset binding. It is not an application server and never reads or
	// transforms corpus text. Its one job is preserving a real 404 for an
	// invalid citation while allowing valid deep links to boot the SPA shell.
	"main": "src/worker.ts",
	"$schema": "./node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
	"name": "glossa",
	"compatibility_date": "2026-08-16",

	// OFF, stated explicitly rather than left to a default or a dashboard
	// toggle, because Workers Cache is the one platform feature here that looks
	// like a fix for this site's cost model and is in fact the same outage
	// `run_worker_first` already caused once.
	//
	// The pitch is exact: "Cloudflare checks the cache before running your
	// Worker", so a crawler re-fetching the same ~5,800 canonical addresses
	// would execute `src/worker.ts` once per address instead of once per
	// request. The billing note underneath it is the part that matters
	// (workers/cache, Pricing):
	//
	//   "When caching is enabled, every request to your Worker is charged at
	//    the standard Workers request rate, including requests that are
	//    normally free: static asset requests."
	//
	// A cache HIT is billed at the standard rate even though the Worker does
	// not run, and — decisively — every corpus chunk, font and script under the
	// `!` negations below stops being free. That is precisely the ~2,240
	// invocations per cold visitor that the negation list was written to
	// eliminate, reintroduced through a different door and with a cache in
	// front of it. There is nothing to gain in exchange: this Worker does no
	// work worth caching. It reads a manifest once per isolate and hands the
	// request back to the asset binding.
	//
	// `enabled: false` is versioned with the deploy (workers/cache,
	// Configuration §Versioned deployments), so it also survives a rollback and
	// cannot be re-enabled behind the repo's back without a deploy that shows
	// up in this file's history.
	"cache": { "enabled": false },

	// The site answers on one hostname, and it is this one. `workers_dev`
	// defaults to true, which would keep glossa.me-f65.workers.dev serving the
	// same corpus from a second address -- harmless while the site asked not to
	// be crawled, and a duplicate-content problem the moment it stopped
	// (docs/decisions.md, Posture: "Indexable, and a duplicate of vatican.va on
	// purpose"). Turning it off is what makes the canonical address canonical in
	// fact rather than by intention.
	//
	// The route is declared here rather than left to the dashboard so that this
	// file, not an account someone else cannot see, is the record of where the
	// worker is reachable. `custom_domain` (not a plain route pattern) is the
	// form that owns the DNS record and the certificate.
	"workers_dev": false,
	"routes": [{ "pattern": "glossacatholica.org", "custom_domain": true }],
	"assets": {
		"directory": "./build",
		"binding": "ASSETS",

		// Reader URLs are slashless canonical paths. The edge worker performs
		// the redirect for navigation requests before handing the SPA shell back;
		// this setting keeps static HTML assets (notably offline.html) consistent.
		"html_handling": "auto-trailing-slash",

		// The worker, rather than a host-wide SPA fallback, chooses which
		// navigation requests receive index.html. That keeps canonical deep
		// links working while a typo such as /catechismus/999999 remains a real 404.
		"not_found_handling": "404-page",

		// Navigations only — NOT the boolean `true`, which is what this was
		// until 2026-08-25 and which is a hard outage waiting on traffic.
		//
		// `true` routes EVERY request through the worker, and every one of those
		// is a billed Worker invocation: all 2,236 corpus JSON chunks, plus JS,
		// CSS, fonts and icons. `src/worker.ts` returns non-navigations to the
		// asset binding on its first line, but the invocation is already spent.
		// A single cold visitor that fills the offline library therefore cost
		// ~2,240 invocations against the free plan's 100,000/day — about fifty
		// first-time readers before the day's budget was gone.
		//
		// The failure past that limit is not graceful, which is the part that
		// makes this urgent rather than merely wasteful: under `run_worker_first`
		// Cloudflare answers 429 instead of falling back to serving the asset
		// (workers/static-assets/billing-and-limitations), so with `true` the
		// whole site — every path — went dark until 00:00 UTC.
		//
		// Scoped this way a cold visit costs ONE invocation, and past the limit
		// the site degrades instead of dying: negative patterns keep serving
		// assets, so corpus text, JS and fonts stay up and only fresh navigations
		// 429. A reader with the service worker already installed sees nothing.
		//
		// The negations are the top level of `build/`, and they are a list rather
		// than a rule because there is no shape that separates them from reader
		// paths — `/scriptura/gen/1` and `/robots.txt` look alike to a glob. A
		// new static file added to `static/` and not added here still WORKS; it
		// just quietly costs an invocation per request, which is exactly the
		// mistake this comment exists to make findable.
		//
		// NOTHING NEEDS ADDING FOR THE DORE PLATES, and the reflex to add
		// `!/plates/*` is the mistake to resist -- there is no such path. The
		// 482 images are content-hashed BUILD assets under `/_app/immutable/`
		// (see `syncPlates` in scripts/sync-corpus.mjs for why they go through
		// Vite rather than into `static/`), so the first negation already
		// covers them -- and so does the zone's rate limiting rule, which
		// excludes `/_app/` by prefix. A chapter with four plates costs the
		// same one invocation as a chapter with none.
		//
		// THE FIRST THREE ARE ALSO THE ZONE'S RATE LIMITING RULE. That rule
		// excludes `/_app/`, `/fonts/` and `/icons/` by prefix -- and verified
		// bots, which is the SEO clause -- so it does not count the offline
		// library's ~2,240-asset burst against the reader who asked for it. It is
		// dashboard state and nothing here can assert the two agree, so changing
		// these three means changing it too -- docs/decisions.md, "The zone has
		// one rate limiting rule". Drift is silent and rate-limits the site's
		// headline feature.
		"run_worker_first": [
			"/*",
			"!/_app/*",
			"!/fonts/*",
			"!/icons/*",
			"!/favicon.ico",
			"!/robots.txt",
			"!/llms.txt",
			"!/manifest.webmanifest",
			"!/service-worker.js",
			"!/corpus-routes.json",
			"!/reference-coverage.json",
			"!/offline.html",
			"!/sitemap.xml",
			"!/og.png",
			// Not just the file that exists. `/.well-known/*` is where every scanner
			// probes -- security.txt, then a dozen paths we will never serve -- and
			// a miss handled here is a static 404 rather than a Worker invocation
			// spent to reach the same answer. The wildcard is the point: negating
			// only security.txt would leave the misses billable.
			"!/.well-known/*"
		]
	},

	// Usage measurement. `/a` is NOT negated above, and must not be: the worker
	// is the only thing that can receive a beacon, and one invocation per
	// reading session is immaterial beside the navigations already billed.
	//
	// The binding is optional in `src/worker.ts`. A deploy without it serves
	// the site normally and drops beacons on the floor, which is the right
	// failure for a statistic and the reason `wrangler dev` needs no database.
	//
	// TWO PIECES OF THIS LIVE IN THE DASHBOARD and nothing here can assert
	// they agree -- docs/decisions.md §Usage measurement is their record:
	//
	//   1. A WAF custom rule (one of the free plan's five) blocking `/a` for
	//      verified bots, non-POST methods and non-same-origin requests.
	//   2. The zone's single rate limiting rule already covers `/a`, because
	//      its expression excludes only /_app/, /fonts/ and /icons/ by prefix
	//      and verified bots -- and a beacon is none of those, rule 1 having
	//      blocked verified bots on /a already. There is no second rate
	//      limiting rule available on this plan, which is why the write ceiling
	//      is enforced in `usage-store.ts` instead.
	//
	// The kill switch is a third custom rule blocking `/a` outright. It is a
	// dashboard change rather than a deploy on purpose: a client-side stop
	// needs the service worker to propagate, and `sw.svelte.ts` documents that
	// an installed PWA can sit on a superseded shell indefinitely.
	//
	// Retention, enforced rather than remembered. `scheduled()` in
	// `src/worker.ts` drops rows past `RETENTION_DAYS`; a policy that only runs
	// when someone types `--prune` is an indeterminate retention period however
	// firmly the constant is written down, and that is the one thing the ANPD
	// cookie guide refuses.
	//
	// 04:10 UTC: off the hour, because every cron on the platform fires on it,
	// and after 00:00 UTC so the day the prune reads is a finished one. One
	// invocation a day against the free plan's 100,000 is not worth a thought;
	// the reason it is declared here rather than in the dashboard is that this
	// file, not an account nobody else can see, should be the record of it.
	"triggers": { "crons": ["10 4 * * *"] },

	// `database_id` comes from `npx wrangler d1 create glossa-usage`; apply the
	// schema with `npx wrangler d1 migrations apply glossa-usage --remote`.
	"d1_databases": [
		{
			"binding": "USAGE",
			"database_name": "glossa-usage",
			"database_id": "a5525a74-9f55-4649-8fec-84b553acdbdc",
			"migrations_dir": "migrations"
		}
	]
}
